documentation anymore. **That becomes the application information architecture.**\n\nThe key architectural shift I see is this: **DevPad should not be another betting engine.** It should initially be a **read/write visualization and interaction client over immutable/timestamped Quant states.** The Quant engine produces the truth objects; React presents them and creates controlled overlays. Baseline remains read-only. Don Delta is a new object. Model Synthesis is another object. Reanalysis produces another state/version; it does not mutate history.\n\nSo at the top level I would structure the React product around five persistent domains:\n\n1. **Slate / Dashboard** — today's games, readiness/status, markets, fair state, edge/EV, uncertainty, last material change.\n2. **Game Workspace** — one matchup, with Baseline / Don Delta / Model Synthesis as explicit views rather than one mashed-together score.\n3. **Evidence Drill-down** — components → variables → calculation/evidence/provenance, including `DIRECT / DERIVED / PROXY / UNAVAILABLE`, stale/missing state, timestamps and source.\n4. **Interaction Layer** — Don adds a read, we classify/validate it, preserve it independently, then intentionally trigger synthesis/reanalysis.\n5. **State History / Replay** — pregame snapshot, later pregame updates, eventual live states, and post-event reconciliation without overwriting anything.\n\nAnd I would **not** begin by wiring live APIs or rebuilding the whole backend. For the first DevPad pass, I want a **frozen MLB fixture adapter** that takes the actual current Terminal output shape and renders it. That gives us something visible immediately and lets the frontend expose holes in the data contract before Nico/implementation starts inventing backend architecture.\n\nThere is old React precedent on the machine—`devpad/web`, Vite + React + TypeScript, with prior Edge Crew board work—but the August `Process 79` implementation carried old EC4 concepts like kill switches, bankroll rules, WNBA feed assumptions, and old grading logic. I am treating that as **code/UI salvage only**, not product semantics. The September 20 current Quant artifacts explicitly define a different interaction model.\n\nThe first screen I want us to earn is therefore very simple conceptually:\n\n> **MLB — Today**  \n> 15 games  \n> each game card shows **status + matchup + MGM market + Quant fair state + edge/EV + uncertainty**  \n> click game → **Baseline**  \n> drill down → components/evidence/provenance  \n> add Don read → **Don Delta** appears beside—not inside—the Baseline  \n> run synthesis → third view appears  \n> original Baseline remains frozen.\n\nThat gives you exactly what you're asking for: **you can finally see the machine instead of reading artifacts and terminal dumps.**\n\nOne other important conclusion from the grounding: the September parent work already requires timestamped analytical states, explicit missingness, Baseline/Don Delta separation, independent totals, provenance, and future live-state preservation. If we build the React state model around those now, MLB can be the reference implementation without hard-coding the application to baseball. NFL/NCAA later plug into the same shell with different sport-specific component trees. memcite\n\n**My next move in this chat should be the DevPad contract, not visual styling:** define the exact frontend object model—`SlateSnapshot`, `EventState`, `MarketState`, `QuantBaseline`, `ComponentState`, `EvidenceItem`, `DonDelta`, `ModelSynthesis`, `ReanalysisState`—and map the frozen Terminal MLB output into it. Once that is clean, we can hand Nico a buildable React surface instead of saying “go make a dashboard.”", "text_chars": 4417, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "99bdaebf-05ca-4b1c-ac75-6033ba2aeb0e", "role": "tool", "role_name": "fu