h implementation → Luce → lane fix → Luce → Chiave.\n- \"Can canonical synthesis execute?\" repeatedly approached from shell, methodology, math core, lane maps, then value surface.\n\n### Confidence decreased despite more work\n\nA major example:\n\nGeneric Model Synthesis shell was green and honest.\n\nThen pursuing canonical execution exposed that the state model did **not actually contain executable human delta magnitudes**.\n\nSo after more work, the system knew more—but the path to canonical registration became **less complete than initially assumed**.\n\nThat is a classic sign of scope outrunning ground truth.\n\n### Certification replaced visible progress\n\nThe period after the Model Synthesis shell became visible produced many:\n\n- handoffs;\n- test counts;\n- rulings;\n- gate verdicts;\n- semantic definitions;\n- re-verifications;\n\nbut **no corresponding new end-user capability appeared in the React DevPad**.\n\n---\n\n# F. ASSUMPTIONS / INVENTED STATE\n\n| Claim | Classification | Finding |\n|---|---|---|\n| Frozen MLB data could drive a React Baseline surface | **VERIFIED** | It did. |\n| Baseline could be deterministic/read-only | **VERIFIED** | Tests and Luce established it. |\n| Original SHA implementation was standards-compliant | **ASSUMED → FALSE** | Luce disproved it. |\n| Don Delta could be implemented separately from Baseline | **VERIFIED** | Built and tested. |\n| `SystemClock()` wiring was replay-safe | **ASSUMED → INCOMPLETE** | Needed pinned RunClock. |\n| Slice-2 five-state validation vocabulary matched Parent authority | **ASSUMED → FALSE** | Required split into validation + verification. |\n| Generic Model Synthesis shell could exist without canonical sport math | **VERIFIED** | It does. |\n| Canonical MLB synthesis could execute from existing Don Delta records | **ASSUMED → FALSE** | Chiave found no magnitude/value producer. |\n| `ecq-synthesis-v1@1.0.0` math could be grounded from existing MLB docs | **SUPPORTED** | Corrado grounded most of it, but source anchor was partly draft/scoped. |\n| Human prose/classification could imply a numeric delta | **UNKNOWN / UNDEFINED** | Corrado explicitly found no authority. |\n| Existing frozen MLB manifest was enough for registration | **ASSUMED → FALSE/INCOMPLETE** | Missing conformance fields. |\n| Weather/park/umpire belonged in MONEYLINE lane map | **ASSUMED → FALSE** | Luce found over-admission. |\n| `days_rest` belonged in TOTALS | **UNKNOWN**, then resolved | Corrado ruled EXCLUDED. |\n| Value materialization was required | **SUPPORTED**, then architecturally frozen | Chiave's refusal made the missing layer explicit. |\n\n---\n\n# G. PROJECT / CONTEXT BLEED\n\nThere **was** context bleed, but not simply because historical material existed.\n\n### Useful context\n\nThe Gate 6 architecture and Parent Quant invariants prevented real mistakes:\n\n- Baseline mutation;\n- sport-specific leakage into Parent;\n- fake deterministic identity;\n- ambient-time replay problems;\n- silent synthesis fallbacks.\n\nThat context had genuine value.\n\n### Harmful causal bleed\n\nThe GCE framework itself increasingly became the work product.\n\nOnce every uncertainty had an obvious seat owner, the session naturally turned into:\n\n**route → handoff → verify → reconcile → route again.**\n\nThat is where framework context began controlling execution rather than supporting it.\n\nSpecific causal examples:\n\n- historical/current Kimi/Nico naming required repeated seat correction;\n- Parent Quant governance became part of even small React implementation conversations;\n- cross-seat routing discipline encouraged semantic questions to become separate sessions/gates;\n- old/draft MLB math artifacts were pulled into a UI implementation thread because canonical synthesis execution was pursued before closing the UI build.\n\nThe biggest bleed was **not unrelated project data**.\n\nIt was **governance depth bleeding into implementation depth**.\n\n---\n\n# H. SLICE AUDIT\n\n| Slice / Pass | Why it existed | Concrete artifact | Necessary? | Could be absorbed? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Slice 1 — Baseline React | Original requested vertical slice | React slate + Baseline drilldown | **Yes** | No |\n| Slice 1 SHA repair | Real certification defect | standards-compliant SHA + tests | **Yes** | Yes, normal fix cycle |\n| Slice 1 re-verification | Confirm SHA repair | Luce PASS | **Yes** | Appropriate one reverify |\n| Slice 2 — Don Delta | Explicitly authorized product expansion | Don Delta view/ledger/workflow | **Yes for expanded scope** | No |\n| Pre-Slice-3 clock/vocab repair | Fix Slice-2 debt | RunClock + canonical vocab | **Yes before synthesis** | **Yes**, could have been Slice-2 closure |\n| Re-verification of repair | Verify bounded repair | 94/94 certification | Reasonable | Could stay one closure pass |\n| Slice 3 — generic synthesis shell | Third product state | real shell/UI/API/ledger | **Yes if synthesis in scope** | No |\n| Slice 3 shell verification | Independent check | 114/114 verified | Reasonable | Normal |\n| Corrado canonical synthesis definition | Fill explicit SPORT GAP | quantitative rule spec | Needed only for canonical execution | **Outside original DevPad ask** |\n| Nico MLB math core | Implement Corrado math | `mlb_ecq_synthesis_v1.ts` | Needed only for canonical execution | Could be separate project/pass |\n| Luce math-core verification | Check math | PASS | Useful | Normal |\n| Lane-map correction | Real discrepancy | corrected 5/7/5 lane sets | Yes if core pursued | Could be same math-core fix cycle |\n| Lane-map re-verification | Confirm correction | 129/129 | Legitimate but small | Yes, could be folded into one final math verification |\n| Chiave shell-seam gate | Registration blocked | refusal verdict | **Legitimate architecture gate** | Could not be coded around |\n| Corrado value-materialization definition | Missing magnitude producer | semantic definition | Needed only because registration kept advancing | New subsystem |\n| Chiave value-materialization freeze | Freeze new layer | A–H contract ruling | Architecturally valid | **Clearly a new build phase**, not original session |\n\nThe decisive observation:\n\n> By the value-materialization freeze, the session was no longer finishing the original React DevPad. It was designing the missing quantitative human-input substrate beneath future canonical synthesis.\n\n---\n\n# I. VERIFICATION AUDIT\n\n## Legitimate defects\n\nLuce found several high-value issues:\n\n- broken SHA-256;\n- incorrect hash self-test;\n- app→test runtime dependency;\n- production clock variance;\n- validation vocabulary mismatch;\n- MONEYLINE over-admission;\n- TOTALS/rest ambiguity;\n- references-only shell unable to supply required numeric values.\n\nThese were real.\n\n## Useful evidence checks\n\nAlso useful:\n\n- Baseline immutability;\n- replay determinism;\n- anti-lookahead;\n- write authority;\n- sport boundary;\n- manifest hash checks;\n- NON-CANON honesty;\n- no hidden fallback;\n- full-path EXPIRED behavior.\n\n## Low-value nook-and-cranny findings\n\nExamples:\n\n- stale `tsconfig.node.ts`;\n- test naming/organization;\n- machine-specific test allowlist paths;\n- dev-server dependency warning in a local trusted build;\n- permanent test placement for an already independently verified EXPIRED path.\n\nThese were not bad observations, but they did not warrant routing weight.\n\n## Findings that triggered too much process\n\nThe clock/vocabulary findings were real, but orchestration converted them into a separate \"pre-Slice-3\" governance phase.\n\nLikewise the lane-map issue was a small bounded numeric correction but became:\n\nLuce → Corrado → Nico → Luce.\n\nThat was procedurally correct by seats, but expensive relative to the actual change.\n\n## Recursive certification\n\nThe session repeatedly re-established global invariants after localized changes.\n\nThe same properties were checked many times:\n\n- Baseline immutable;\n- three