o new decision was actually needed;\n- repeated full-system certification language after tiny local patches;\n- stale handoff statements saying Don/Coda decisions were pending after those decisions had already been made;\n- duplicated prompts that asked seats to rediscover current state already established in the immediate prior turn.\n\n## UNKNOWN\n\n- whether the newly frozen value-materialization layer is ultimately required in exactly its current shape;\n- whether all proposed successor-manifest fields/version choices are the final best fit;\n- whether explicit signed human magnitude is the long-term user-product design Don actually wants.\n\nThose were not yet proven by runnable product use.\n\n---\n\n# N. ROOT-CAUSE CANDIDATES\n\n### 1. **Coda orchestration drift — HIGH evidence**\n\nThe parent conversation repeatedly converted local findings into new routed phases.\n\nThis is the strongest candidate.\n\n### 2. **Unclear session-level stop condition — HIGH**\n\nIndividual tasks had stops.\n\nThe parent session did not.\n\nEvery newly exposed seam became \"the next thing.\"\n\n### 3. **Overslicing — HIGH**\n\nA bounded product build became:\n\nSlice 1 → repair → verification → Slice 2 → pre-Slice-3 → repair → verification → Slice 3 → verification → Corrado → Nico → Luce → Corrado → Nico → Luce → Chiave → Corrado → Chiave.\n\n### 4. **Verifier recursion — MEDIUM-HIGH**\n\nLuce mostly behaved correctly.\n\nThe recursion came from **what orchestration did with Luce's findings**.\n\n### 5. **Missing parent-session framing/header/footer — MEDIUM-HIGH**\n\nNo persistent session contract constrained the Coda-level conversation.\n\nThe seats were better framed than the orchestration.\n\n### 6. **Handoff information loss/staleness — MEDIUM**\n\nA concrete example occurred when later artifacts still described Don/Coda decisions as pending even though Don had already accepted them in this conversation.\n\nThat forced re-grounding and increased doubt.\n\n### 7. **Builder interruption — MEDIUM**\n\nDots3's stalls created an early interruption.\n\nLater, builders were repeatedly paused at legitimate authority boundaries, but the cumulative effect fragmented implementation continuity.\n\n### 8. **Project/context overloading — MEDIUM**\n\nFramework doctrine and Parent Quant history were useful but became too operationally present in a task whose immediate user goal was a runnable React surface.\n\n### 9. **Model quality — LOW as primary cause**\n\nThere were model/runtime issues—especially Dots3 looping—but Big Pickle produced strong implementation work.\n\nThe dominant failure was not that models could not execute.\n\nIt was that **the operating frame allowed the task to keep recursively expanding**.\n\n---\n\n# O. EARLY WARNING SIGNALS\n\n### 1. Original visible artifact already existed, but the session did not close\n\nOnce Slice 1 was visible and verified, that was the first opportunity to terminate the original task cleanly.\n\n### 2. Repeated phrases equivalent to \"one more slice\"\n\nThe progression:\n\n**Slice 2 → pre-Slice-3 → Slice 3 → canonical transform → value materialization**\n\nis the clearest warning pattern.\n\n### 3. Repeated verification without a new visible artifact\n\nAfter the Slice 3 shell, multiple seat cycles occurred without a new Don-usable capability.\n\nThat should have fired a circuit breaker.\n\n### 4. Architecture appeared inside what had begun as a DevPad build\n\nChiave's eventual refusal was legitimate.\n\nBut the moment a new architectural layer was necessary to make canonical synthesis executable, the correct interpretation should have been:\n\n> **This is a new work item, not another fix inside the current DevPad session.**\n\n### 5. Multiple seats began questioning premises thought to be settled\n\nExamples:\n\n- synthesis semantics;\n- lane membership;\n- magnitude source;\n- shell value surface;\n- manifest conformance.\n\nThat is evidence that the current phase boundary was wrong.\n\n### Session-header signal\n\nYes: the lack of a persistent **Coda session header/footer** correlated with drift.\n\nSeat sessions had increasing discipline while the parent orchestration accumulated unresolved branches.\n\nThat inversion is notable.\n\n---\n\n# P. FINAL POSTMORTEM\n\n**SESSION STATUS:**  \n**PARTIALLY LOOP-CONTAMINATED**\n\n**ORIGINAL ASK:**  \nBuild a usable React Edge Crew Quant DevPad over frozen MLB state, starting with a slate, one-game Baseline view, and drill-down/provenance.\n\n**ACTUAL DELIVERABLE:**  \nA working three-view React Quant DevPad with certified Baseline and Don Delta, a verified generic Model Synthesis shell, plus a separately verified MLB synthesis math core that is still not canonically registered because the certified state lacks an authoritative numeric human-overlay value surface.\n\n**LOOP STARTED:**  \nAfter the generic Model Synthesis shell was already runnable and correctly exposed the missing canonical sport transform as a loud SPORT GAP, when the same DevPad session continued into canonical MLB synthesis semantics and registration rather than closing the product-build phase.\n\n**PRIMARY FAILURE MECHANISM:**  \nCoda orchestration repeatedly converted legitimate verification findings and downstream semantic gaps into additional slices/gates instead of closing a coherent runnable build and moving new concerns into separate bounded work.\n\n**TIME / COMPLEXITY INFLATION:**  \nSubstantial. The session moved from a normal React vertical-slice build into quantitative methodology, lane semantics, architecture gates, manifest conformance, and finally design of a new value-materialization subsystem.\n\n**SALVAGEABLE STATE:**  \nHigh. Most code and several verification findings are valid; the contamination is primarily in sequencing, routing, and process expansion rather than wholesale bad implementation.\n\n**FIRST CIRCUIT BREAKER THAT SHOULD HAVE FIRED:**  \nOnce the first runnable Baseline DevPad—and later the verified generic Model